## Try these (M5 — toggle commands)

Place your cursor on a task and press one of the keybindings below. Edits
land as a single `WorkspaceEdit` — one Ctrl+Z reverts the whole operation.
Multi-cursor works too; the toggle applies once per unique line.

| Keybinding   | Command                                       | Behavior |
|--------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------|
| `Alt+A`      | `tsk.toggleTodo`                              | Wrap a plain/empty line as `[ ]` with `@id` + `@created`; unwrap an empty todo back to its indent; **or** promote a markered-but-no-`@id` todo by filling in the missing metadata. |
| `Alt+S`      | `tsk.toggleInprogress`                        | Swap marker to `[/]` + `@started`; toggling again reverts to `[ ]`. |
| `Alt+C`      | `tsk.toggleCompleted`                         | Same shape with `[x]` + `@completed`. |
| `Alt+X`      | `tsk.toggleCancelled`                         | Same shape with `[!]` + `@cancelled`. |
| `Alt+N`      | `tsk.toggleNote`                              | Wrap/unwrap as `[n]`, like `toggleTodo`; promotes a markered-but-no-`@id` note the same way. |
| `Alt+M`      | `tsk.toggleMoved`                             | Picker → `[>]` + `@movedTo` + `@moved`. Empty submission writes only `@moved` ("moved elsewhere, no target"). |
| `Alt+R`      | `tsk.toggleRelatedTo`                         | Absent → picker → write; present → silently remove. |
| `Alt+D`      | `tsk.toggleDependsOn`                         | Same shape with `@dependsOn`. |
| `Alt+P`      | `tsk.toggleParent`                            | Same shape with `@parent`. |
| `Alt+1/2/3`  | `tsk.toggleP1` / `P2` / `P3`                  | Toggle `@priority:N` (switching levels overwrites in one step). |
| `` Alt+` ``  | `tsk.copyTaskId`                              | Copy the current task's `@id` to the system clipboard. |

Cursor on any of the lines below and try the matching keybinding:

- [ ] flip me to in-progress with Alt+S <!-- @id:d5try1 -->
- [/] flip me back to todo with Alt+S <!-- @id:d5try2 @started:2026-05-27T09:00:00+08:00 -->
- [ ] complete me with Alt+C <!-- @id:d5try3 -->
- [x] uncomplete me with Alt+C <!-- @id:d5try4 @completed:2026-05-27T09:00:00+08:00 -->
- [ ] cancel me with Alt+X <!-- @id:d5try5 -->
- [ ] move me with Alt+M — leave the picker empty to mark as moved-elsewhere <!-- @id:d5try6 -->
- [ ] add a relatedTo with Alt+R <!-- @id:d5try7 -->
- [ ] add a dependsOn with Alt+D <!-- @id:d5try8 -->
- [ ] add a parent with Alt+P <!-- @id:d5try9 -->
- [ ] toggle me to P1 with Alt+1, then to P2 with Alt+2 (overwrite) <!-- @id:d5try10 -->
- [ ] copy my `@id` with `` Alt+` `` <!-- @id:d5try11 -->
- [ ] promote me with Alt+A — I have no `@id` yet
- [n] promote me with Alt+N — I have no `@id` yet
- [x] promote me via the lightbulb (Ctrl+.) — Alt+A wouldn't fire here because the marker isn't `[ ]`

**Keybinding caveats.** `Alt+1/2/3` are globally bound to "Focus N-th Editor
Group"; the `when: editorLangId == 'tsk'` clause makes our binding win inside
`.tsk` files only. `` Alt+` `` uses the backtick key, which varies by keyboard
layout (UK / DE / FR users may need to rebind via Keyboard Shortcuts).

**Code actions (M19/C).** Place the cursor on any markered task missing
`@id` (including non-`[ ]` markers) and press Ctrl+. (or click the lightbulb).
A "Tsk: Add missing id + created" (or "Tsk: Add missing id" if `@created`
already exists) quick-fix surfaces. Applying it writes the same metadata
Alt+A would, but works regardless of marker — handy for promoting a
pre-existing `[x]` task you imported from somewhere else.

**Broken-reference quick-fixes (M20/C).** When a task has `@parent`,
`@dependsOn`, or `@relatedTo` pointing at an id with no canonical
occurrence in the workspace, you'll see a yellow squiggle on the line.
Open the lightbulb and pick **"Tsk: Remove broken @<key>"** to drop the
bad reference, or **"Tsk: Replace @<key> via picker…"** to open the task
picker and write a new id. Try the lines below — both reference ghost
ids that don't exist anywhere in the workspace.

- [ ] orphan with broken @parent <!-- @id:m20-demo-orphan @parent:m20-demo-ghost -->
- [ ] orphan with broken @dependsOn <!-- @id:m20-demo-orphan-dep @dependsOn:m20-demo-ghost-dep -->

## Try Enter / Tab here (M7)

Place your cursor on one of these tasks and press Enter, Tab, or Shift+Tab.
Edits land as a single `WorkspaceEdit` — one Ctrl+Z reverts everything.

| Key       | Effect on a task line                                                                  |
|-----------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `Enter`   | At end of content → continue the list (new empty task below). Mid-content → split (metadata pins to line 1). On an empty task → outdent (or remove at col 0). |
| `Tab`     | On an empty task → indent one level. Otherwise → default editor Tab.                   |
| `Shift+Tab` | On any indented task → outdent one level.                                            |

When suggest / snippet UI is visible, Enter and Tab go back to their default
roles (accept suggestion, advance snippet placeholder) — the `when` clause
on the keybinding gives priority back to VSCode in those cases.

- [ ] press Enter at the end of this line to continue the list <!-- @id:m7try1 -->
- [ ] no metadata here — Enter still continues the list, just without pinning
- [ ] press Enter between `foo` and `bar` to see the split with metadata pinned to line 1 <!-- @id:m7try2 -->
- [ ]
- [ ] ↑ that's an empty task — Enter on it removes the marker (col 0) or outdents (col > 0)
    - [ ] indented empty task — Enter outdents <!-- @id:m7try3 -->
        - [ ] deeply nested — repeated Shift+Tab walks back to column 0 <!-- @id:m7try4 -->
- [ ] Tab on this content task is a no-op (defers to default editor Tab)
- [x] Enter at end of a completed task still continues with a fresh `[ ]` marker

## Tags (M8)

Tags participate in two surfaces beyond M2's parser:

- **`#`-triggered autocompletion** — start typing `#` inside any `.tsk`
  document and a CompletionItemProvider surfaces every tag known to the
  workspace. Items come from two sources, merged into one list:
  - declared in `${workspaceFolder}/.vscode/tsk/tags.yml` (configurable
    via `tsk.tags.path`), and
  - discovered from `.tsk` files via the M3 cache, plus all their
    implicit `/`-separated parents (so `#project/tsk` contributes
    `#project` to the list automatically).
- **`Alt+T` find-all-tasks-by-tag** — opens a QuickPick of every known
  tag (searchable by name, task count, and yaml description), then drops
  you into VSCode's built-in Find-in-Files Search Editor with `#<tag>`
  already populated and scoped to `*.tsk`. Ctrl+Click any result to jump
  to the line. Each row shows a task count: `5 tasks · <description>`.
  The count is **hierarchical** — `#project` reports every task under it
  (including `#project/tsk` ones), matching what the `#project` search
  returns. A tag declared in `tags.yml` but carried by no task shows
  `0 tasks`, so you can see it exists but is empty.

The sibling `docs/.vscode/tsk/tags.yml` ships with descriptions for
every tag below, so the dev host has autocompletion + find-by-tag
working against this file out of the box.

`tags.yml` schema — both forms are accepted in the same file:

```yml
<tag>: <description>            # string shorthand
<tag>:                           # object form
    description: <description>
    parent: <tag>
```

`parent` is an explicit override; implicit parents (`project/tsk` →
`project`) are computed automatically regardless of what `tags.yml`
says.

