### Try Alt+T

Place your cursor anywhere in this file and press `Alt+T`. The
QuickPick lists every tag (including implicit parents like `project`
and `inventory/homelab`), each annotated with its hierarchical task
count. Note that `project` and `inventory/homelab` show non-zero
counts even though no task carries them literally — they inherit the
counts of their `/`-children. Pick one → Search Editor opens with
results pre-populated.

**Keybinding caveats.** `Alt+T` follows the same convention as the
other Alt-letter bindings — gated to `editorLangId == 'tsk' &&
editorTextFocus`, so it only fires when you're inside a `.tsk`
editor. Third-party extensions with an equally specific `when` clause
could shadow it; some EU keyboard layouts (where `Alt+letter` produces
a typed character) may need a rebind via Keyboard Shortcuts.

## Codelens (M9)

Every task with relationship metadata grows code lenses showing forward
edges (`parent: <id>`, `dependsOn: <id>`, `relatedTo: <id>`) and inverse
edges (`children: N`, `dependents: N`, `related: N`). Each title is
prefixed with a codicon glyph hinting the relationship type — the
current mapping lives in `CODICONS` in `src/lib/codelens-logic.ts`,
intended to graduate to a user setting later. Forward lenses navigate
on click; inverse lenses open a peek view of every source pointing
here. Dangling forward edges — where the referenced `@id` doesn't exist
anywhere in the workspace — render with a warning glyph and a
"(missing)" suffix, and pop an info toast on click.

Lenses appear only on the **canonical occurrence** of an `@id` (the
lex-lowest `(file, line)` in the workspace). A duplicate-`@id` task
gets a diagnostic squiggle on its line instead, pointing at the
canonical winner. The graph layer's `tsk` Output log line for each
duplicate is the chronological record.

Note on rendering: code-lens font is controlled by VSCode's
`editor.codeLensFontFamily` setting, which in practice does not always
match the editor font even when the option doc implies it should. We
deliberately don't customize per-lens fonts — adding a `$(codicon)`
prefix or other markup wouldn't help, only changing the global
setting does.

